From 4d774f90e8d0ba48493b24b1bc728bfd3b6b27e2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Craterbrain <58347640+Craterbrain@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 22:51:45 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 66/75] PlaitOS: Peek Display, a Motorola-style ambient display

New SystemUI feature (design: plait-os docs/peek-display-design.md), off
unless Settings.Secure plait_peek_display is 1:

- A notification pulse (or a breath, below) shows a black overlay with only
  the clock, the date and one icon per app with unseen notifications, instead
  of the stock notification card. Other pulses (fingerprint, pickup, ...) are
  left alone, and on under-display fingerprint phones the window stops above
  the sensor so screen-off fingerprint unlock keeps working
- Touch and hold an icon: that app's newest notification appears (redacted
  exactly as the lock screen would, getRedactionType); slide up to open it
  through ActivityStarter.startPendingIntentDismissingKeyguard (unlock first,
  click reported for auto-cancel as on the lock screen), slide down to dismiss
  it as an AOD dismissal (only if dismissable); the pulse is extended while
  held. No icons when lock screen notifications are off
- Breathing: while unseen notifications remain, re-pulse every
  plait_peek_breathing_interval seconds (default 30) for
  plait_peek_breathing_duration minutes (default 10) after the newest one,
  via DozeTriggers' pulse request (which does the pocket proximity check);
  stops when the screen turns on. plait_peek_breathing=0 turns it off
- Silent, minimized, ongoing and foreground-service notifications are left
  out; the content moves a few dp per pulse against burn-in

DozeServiceHost gets a small PulseListener hook (pulse started with its
reason, pulse finished); PeekDisplayController is a CoreStartable.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
